# Copy to .env and fill in. Never commit .env.
#
# Everything in here is for running things on your own machine. The scheduled
# pipeline reads none of it: on your team's Airflow, settings come from Airflow
# Variables and every password comes out of Key Vault at the moment a task
# needs it. That is deliberate, and it is why there is no production credential
# anywhere in this file. If you ever find yourself pasting one in, stop and ask,
# because the design says you should not have to.
#
# Nothing below has to be typed from memory either. Sign in once:
#
#   az login
#
# and each secret has the command that prints it, next to the setting it fills.
# Replace <x> with your team letter throughout. If a command returns
# "SecretNotFound", the secret has not been created for your team yet: ask,
# rather than working around it with a credential you found somewhere else.

# --- Source API -------------------------------------------------------------
# The API your pipeline fetches from. Arbeitnow needs no key and is a good
# first target while you get the shape right. Replace it with your source.
SOURCE_API_URL=https://www.arbeitnow.com/api/job-board-api
# Names the folder your files land in, so two sources never overwrite each
# other. Lowercase, no spaces.
SOURCE_NAME=postings

# Where your own runs write. Two containers, and the split is enforced: the
# scheduled pipeline writes `landing`, which you can only read, and you write
# `dev`, which only you and your teammates can write. Put your name in the
# prefix so two of you never overwrite each other.
LANDING_CONTAINER=dev
LANDING_PREFIX=your-name

# --- Landing zone -----------------------------------------------------------
# Your team's storage account. The ingestion job writes raw files into its
# `landing` container, and Unity Catalog shows the same files at
# /Volumes/<catalog>/landing/raw, so dbt reads them without knowing they are
# blobs. Your own runs write the `dev` container, which appears alongside it
# at /Volumes/<catalog>/landing/dev.
#
# There is no key and no connection string. The job authenticates as itself:
# your `az login` locally, its managed identity in Azure. If you are asked for
# a connection string somewhere, you are on the wrong path.
#
#   az storage account list -g rg-hyf-fp-team-<x> --query "[0].name" -o tsv
STORAGE_ACCOUNT=

# --- Databricks -------------------------------------------------------------
# Your team's catalog, and none of the other teams'. Inside it you can make
# and own your own dev_ schemas, and read the ones the scheduled run
# publishes. dbt builds here; the ingestion job only names it in a log line.
# It is named after your team, so team-b builds in team_b.
DATABRICKS_CATALOG=team_<x>
# The SQL warehouse, shared by all three teams and sized 2X-Small. Sharing the
# compute changes nothing about who can see what: permissions live on the
# catalog, so your queries still reach your catalog and no one else's.
DATABRICKS_HTTP_PATH=/sql/1.0/warehouses/0aae52a375e34214
DATABRICKS_HOST=https://adb-7405619530719547.7.azuredatabricks.net

# The schema dbt builds into, including the enrichment model. Give
# each teammate their own while you are developing, so two people running dbt
# do not overwrite each other. The scheduled run uses `analytics`.
DBT_SCHEMA=dev_yourname

# What dbt reads. Must match LANDING_PREFIX above, or you will build models
# from somebody else's files. `uv run python -m src.ingestion.pipeline` prints the exact
# value to use as its last line.
LANDING_PATH=/Volumes/<your catalog>/landing/dev/your-name/postings

# Your own personal access token, exactly as in Week 13: your name in the top
# bar, Settings, Developer, Access tokens, Generate new token. dbt reads it,
# and so does the publish step, so one value covers everything
# you run locally.
#
# It is yours, not your team's. `select session_user()` will show your email,
# which is what makes the Databricks audit log worth reading. Treat it like a
# database password: it goes in .env and nowhere else, and it belongs in no
# Slack message and no LLM prompt.
DATABRICKS_TOKEN=

# The team's service principal is not here on purpose. It lives in Key Vault,
# Airflow uses it for the scheduled run, and it can write the schemas you
# cannot. Nothing you run on your machine needs it.

# --- Backend database -------------------------------------------------------
# You own the `analytics` schema and publish marts into it. The backend owns
# `app` and you can read it. Neither side can write to the other's, which is
# enforced by two roles rather than by agreement.
#
# Two ways to fill this in, and either is fine while you are developing.
#
# Against your own Postgres in Docker. `scripts/db-setup.py` at the repository
# root creates the database, the schemas and the roles, and prints each
# password once:
#
#   pip install "psycopg[binary]"
#   python ../scripts/db-setup.py --host localhost --port 5432 \
#     --admin-user admin --admin-password password
#
# Or against your team's real database, using the development role. Its
# password is in Key Vault, so nobody has to send it to you:
#
#   az keyvault secret show --vault-name kv-hyf-data \
#     --name fp-pg-analytics-dev-team-<x> --query value -o tsv
#
# That role owns the development schema and can only read the one the
# scheduled run publishes. Point this at the production role and the run stops
# with a permission error, which is the boundary doing its job rather than
# something to route around.
BACKEND_PG_HOST=localhost
BACKEND_PG_PORT=5432
BACKEND_PG_DB=project_db
BACKEND_PG_USER=analytics_user
BACKEND_PG_PASSWORD=                  # db-setup.py prints it, or Key Vault above
BACKEND_PG_PUBLISH_SCHEMA=analytics
# The local container has no TLS certificate; Azure requires one.
BACKEND_PG_SSLMODE=prefer

# --- Running the whole DAG on your machine (optional) -----------------------
# Everything above is enough to run the three steps by hand. Set these as well
# and `astro dev start` in data/airflow runs the real DAG: it starts the same
# Container Apps job the scheduled run starts, then builds dbt and publishes,
# all from your machine. That catches the things a hand-run cannot, like a task
# that works in your shell and not in the container.
#
# There is no Airflow Variable to create. The DAG reads a Variable first and
# falls back to the environment, and Astro passes this file in, so a value here
# reaches the tasks with nothing to click.
#
# The job to start. The dev one, which lands in the `dev` container: your
# LANDING_PATH above must point at the prefix it writes, or dbt builds from a
# folder nothing wrote to.
ACA_INGEST_JOB=job-fp-ingest-dev
#
#   az containerapp job show -g rg-hyf-fp-team-<x> -n job-fp-ingest-dev \
#     --query id -o tsv
#
# prints both of the next two inside one resource id.
AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION=
AZURE_RESOURCE_GROUP=rg-hyf-fp-team-<x>
#
# Who your laptop is when it asks Azure to start that job. On the team VM the
# machine has an identity of its own and none of this is set, which is why the
# scheduled run needs no password anywhere. Here there is no such identity, so
# the credential chain falls through to these three.
#
# This principal exists only for this. It can start that one job and can do
# nothing else: it has no access to the catalog, the storage account or the
# database. It is deliberately not the team's service principal, which Airflow
# uses for the scheduled run and which can write the schemas you cannot. If
# somebody offers you that one to "just get it working", the answer is no.
#
#   az keyvault secret show --vault-name kv-hyf-data \
#     --name fp-devrun-client-id-team-<x> --query value -o tsv
#   az keyvault secret show --vault-name kv-hyf-data \
#     --name fp-devrun-client-secret-team-<x> --query value -o tsv
#   az account show --query tenantId -o tsv
#
# Commented out on purpose. An empty AZURE_CLIENT_ID is worse than a missing
# one: the credential chain reads it as "use this user-assigned identity" and
# fails with "client_id should be the id of a Microsoft Entra application",
# which says nothing about this file. Left absent, a laptop with nothing
# configured fails in a second with a message that names every credential it
# tried. Uncomment the three and fill them in when you want the local DAG.
# AZURE_TENANT_ID=
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
# AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET=                # treat like a password: .env and nowhere else
